  "$comment": "Golden frames recorded against the pinned kernel. The wire protocol carries no version field, so a method name surviving an upgrade proves nothing about its frames — replaying these through the normalizers is the only check that does. The downlink is a WebSocket (a plain GET answers 426 Upgrade Required) and every frame arrives inside a `server-request` envelope; the first mux entry is captured verbatim from a live kernel and the rest are wrapped in the same shape. There is no automated recorder — the verbatim entry was captured by hand against a real installed `dsh` binary (unary calls over `POST /api/<method>`, the mux frame over `openRuntimeDownlink()` against `/api/events.mux`). When the pin moves, repeat that against the new version, diff the result against this file by hand, and read the diff before accepting it — an untested recorder script would be one more thing to trust without ever having been run against a real bump.",
